{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB09096 - MONDO_4980",
  "verbalization": "Benzoyl peroxide is implicated in the treatment of atopic eczema through its indication to dermatitis, atopic, suggesting a potential role in the pathophysiology of the condition. The evidence is supported by its association with crisaborole, dupilumab, and pimecrolimus, all of which are indicated for atopic eczema, indicating a shared therapeutic target. These drugs are typically used to modulate immune responses, reduce inflammation, and restore skin barrier function, which are key mechanisms in atopic eczema. The presence of multiple indications pointing to atopic eczema suggests a plausible mechanistic link, though the exact pathway remains to be elucidated. The integration of these indications supports the hypothesis that benzoyl peroxide may exert a therapeutic effect through a mechanism that is not yet fully understood, but is likely related to its ability to modulate inflammatory processes or enhance skin barrier function.",
